@sanjayuvacha  Sanjay Hegde Sir, I have been reflecting seriously on the entire SIR debate, the criticism of Prime Minister Narendra Modi, and the deeper relationship between migration, citizenship and human dignity. I believe that Rekha Bhardwaj Ma’am’s Kajri tradition, Kachauri Gali, Professor B.S. Chimni’s scholarship on international refugee law, and Jawaharlal Nehru’s humanitarian imagination together offer a remarkable framework for understanding this controversy. Rekha Ma’am gives us the emotional vocabulary of separation, migration and belonging, while Professor Chimni provides the international legal vocabulary of sovereignty, displacement and refugee protection. I remember arguing before the Delhi High Court for an Afghan woman who had not met her husband for fifteen years and whose visa application remained unanswered. I invoked Kachauri Gali because the human history of migration cannot be reduced to passports, borders and administrative files. Nehru's India never acceded to the 1951 Refugee Convention or its 1967 Protocol, yet our civilisation continues to invoke Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam. Is this not a profound political paradox, Sir? The real question is whether a State can demand documentary certainty without recognising the historical uncertainties of human migration.

Sir, this is also why I find the political debate over SIR, Nitish Kumar's record on reservation and promotion, allegations of casteism, political intentions, and the language of victimhood increasingly inadequate when treated as substitutes for constitutional reasoning. Political intentions may be debated, but constitutional consequences must be examined. Refugee protection, citizenship and electoral eligibility are legally distinct; nevertheless, Articles 14 and 21 require us to take fairness, non-arbitrariness and human dignity seriously, while Article 326 governs electoral eligibility. My criticism of Prime Minister Narendra Modi is therefore not simply personal or partisan. It asks whether the government's administrative practices reflect the civilisational philosophy it invokes. Rekha Bhardwaj Ma’am teaches us how separation feels; Professor Chimni teaches us how international law understands displacement; Nehru reminds us of the humanitarian aspirations of independent India; and the Constitution requires us to distinguish lawful verification from arbitrary exclusion. Before reducing SIR to political slogans, accusations of casteism or competing claims of victimhood, we must understand the human consequences of administrative power. Sir, that is the criticism of the Prime Minister I wish to place before you: Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam must be tested not merely in speeches but in the lived experiences of people seeking recognition, reunion and dignity? @rekha_bhardwaj @sanjayuvacha @narendramodi @Profdilipmandal @ECISVEEP 

